Step 1 – Check Kubernetes cluster
kubectl get nodes

Step 2 – Create a Deployment
kubectl create deployment myapp --image=YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/flask-app:v1


Step 3 – Check Deployment
kubectl get deployments


Step 4 – Check Pods
kubectl get pods


Wait until the Pod shows Running and 1/1.
if not then do 
(Open your Killercoda terminal and copy these commands:
kubectl delete deployment myapp --ignore-not-found=true
kubectl delete service myapp --ignore-not-found=true


Now check your Kubernetes cluster:
kubectl get nodes


Expected output should show:
controlplane   Ready)

Step 5 – Create the Kubernetes Deployment

kubectl create deployment myapp --image=hanuel018/flask-docker-app:latest


Expected output:
deployment.apps/myapp created


Step 6 – Check the Deployment
kubectl get deployments


Step 7 – Check the Pod
kubectl get pods


Wait until the Pod shows:
READY   STATUS
1/1     Running


Step 8 – Create a NodePort Service
Copy and paste:
kubectl expose deployment myapp --type=NodePort --port=5000 --target-port=5000


Expected output:
service/myapp exposed


Step 9 – Check the Service
kubectl get services


You should see myapp with type NodePort.

Step 10 – Check Deployment details
kubectl describe deployment myapp


Step 11 – Access the Flask application
Run this command:
kubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 service/myapp 8082:5000


Expected output:
Forwarding from 0.0.0.0:8082 -> 5000


Keep the terminal running.
Step 12 – Open the website
In Killercoda:
1. Open Traffic Port Accessor.
2. Select Host 1.
3. Select Custom Ports.
4. Enter 8082.
5. Open the generated HTTP URL.
Expected website output
Hello


This confirms your Flask application is running through Kubernetes.


Step 12 – Stop port forwarding
Return to the Killercoda terminal and press:

Ctrl + C
Step 11 – Delete the Service
kubectl delete service myapp


Expected:
service "myapp" deleted


Step 13 – Delete the Deployment
kubectl delete deployment myapp


Expected:
deployment.apps "myapp" deleted


Step 14 – Verify deletion
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl get pods


Expected: No application resources remain in the default namespace.
